+# Coupled status
+
+`spiral-coder coupled-status --run-dir PATH` reads a native coupled-run directory and prints one JSON status object. It makes no provider calls, runs no tools, and does not modify the run. Relative paths are resolved from the caller's current directory.
+
+The installed npm entry forwards the same command and arguments:
+
+```sh
+spiral-coder coupled-status --run-dir ./path/to/run
+npx spiral-coder coupled-status --run-dir ./path/to/run
+```
+
+`wire_requests` counts every durable ledger request. `task_requests` counts requests whose `charge` is `task`; omitted `charge` uses the native task default. Failed and in-flight attempts count too. Text logs and report prose are not consulted.
+
+An interrupted run may have an unfinished ledger. If its ledger or one of its pending directory/state/file components is absent, usage is unknown and the output uses `null` for pending usage and aggregate used/remaining totals; it never reports absent usage as zero. An existing malformed ledger or a symlink in a manifest/ledger path is rejected.
+
+The `status` field records the native manifest (`completed`, `stopped`, or `running`). It is an operational record, not independent verification, qualification, or promotion authorization.
+
+Completed synthetic example from `scripts.status_fixture.make`:
+
+```json
+{"version":1,"status":"completed","stop_reason":"completed","iterations":[{"iteration":1,"task_id":"t1","termination":"completed","task_requests":1,"wire_requests":1,"changed_files":["src/lib.rs"],"newly_verified":["cargo test"]}],"pending":null,"requests":{"task_used":1,"wire_used":1,"task_limit":214,"wire_limit":213,"task_remaining":213,"wire_remaining":212}}
+```
